-- Q1: medium / L3 — Net unrealized appreciation (NUA) strategy for employer stock
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Colton Reed, age 62, retires with $300,000 in employer stock (cost basis $80,000) inside his 401(k). His CPA recommends a lump-sum distribution of the stock in-kind rather than rolling it to an IRA. Applying the net unrealized appreciation rules under IRC Section 402(e)(4), what is the tax treatment of this distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Colton pays ordinary income tax on the $80,000 cost basis at distribution; the $220,000 NUA is taxed as long-term capital gain when the stock is later sold", "The entire $300,000 is taxed as ordinary income in the year of distribution", "The entire $300,000 is taxed as long-term capital gain because it was held more than one year in the plan", "Colton pays no tax at distribution; the entire $300,000 is taxed as long-term capital gain at sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 402(e)(4) provides that net unrealized appreciation on employer securities distributed in a lump sum is excluded from gross income at distribution. Only the cost basis ($80,000) is taxed as ordinary income. The $220,000 NUA is deferred and taxed as long-term capital gain when sold, regardless of holding period post-distribution.
Wrong (B): This ignores the NUA exclusion, which specifically prevents the appreciation from being taxed as ordinary income at distribution.
Wrong (C): The cost basis portion ($80,000) is always ordinary income, not capital gain. Only the NUA qualifies for capital gain treatment.
Wrong (D): The cost basis is taxable as ordinary income at distribution; tax deferral applies only to the NUA portion.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Qualified charitable distribution (QCD) vs standard deduction strategy
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$CPA Rita Fontaine advises Mildred Hayes, age 74, who takes the standard deduction ($16,550 for single filers age 65+) and donates $8,000 annually to her church. Mildred's traditional IRA balance is $400,000 and her 2026 RMD is $16,000. Rita compares two strategies: (1) take the full RMD as cash and donate $8,000 separately, or (2) direct $8,000 as a qualified charitable distribution and take the remaining $8,000 as a taxable RMD. Evaluate which strategy minimizes Mildred's AGI.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both strategies produce the same AGI because the charitable donation offsets the RMD either way", "Strategy 2 is superior because the $8,000 QCD satisfies part of the RMD while being excluded from AGI, reducing AGI by $8,000 compared to Strategy 1", "Strategy 1 is superior because the standard deduction already exceeds the charitable donation, making the QCD unnecessary", "Strategy 2 is inferior because QCD amounts cannot satisfy RMD obligations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 408(d)(8), a QCD up to $105,000 per year is excluded from gross income and counts toward the RMD. In Strategy 2, only $8,000 is included in AGI versus $16,000 in Strategy 1. Since Mildred takes the standard deduction, the $8,000 cash donation in Strategy 1 provides no additional tax benefit.
Wrong (A): A standard deduction taxpayer receives no benefit from itemized charitable deductions, so the cash donation does not offset the RMD income.
Wrong (C): The standard deduction does not reduce AGI — the QCD directly reduces AGI, which can affect other AGI-dependent tax provisions.
Wrong (D): QCDs explicitly satisfy RMD obligations under IRC Section 408(d)(8)(A).$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — IRA contribution deadline
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$What is the deadline for making an IRA contribution for a given tax year?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["December 31 of the tax year", "October 15 of the following year if the taxpayer files an extension", "April 15 of the following year, regardless of filing extensions", "March 15 of the following year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 219(f)(3) provides that IRA contributions for a tax year may be made up to the due date of the return, not including extensions. This is April 15 of the following year. Filing an extension does not extend the IRA contribution deadline.
Wrong (A): December 31 is the deadline for many tax-year elections but not IRA contributions, which are allowed through April 15.
Wrong (B): Filing extensions extend the time to file but do not extend the IRA contribution deadline.
Wrong (D): March 15 is the filing deadline for certain entity returns such as partnerships and S corporations, not the IRA contribution deadline.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Investment risk in defined benefit plan
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$In a defined benefit pension plan, who bears the investment risk for plan assets?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The employee, because benefit amounts depend on investment performance", "The PBGC, which guarantees all investment losses during plan operations", "A shared arrangement between employer and employee based on years of service", "The employer, because the promised benefit is fixed regardless of investment returns"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ERISA and IRC Section 412, a defined benefit plan promises a specified retirement benefit calculated by formula based on salary and years of service. The employer must fund any shortfall if investments underperform, bearing all investment risk.
Wrong (A): In a defined benefit plan, the employee's benefit is determined by formula, not investment returns. Investment risk falls entirely on the employer.
Wrong (B): The PBGC insures benefits up to statutory limits if a plan terminates, but does not guarantee investment losses during normal plan operations.
Wrong (C): There is no shared risk arrangement in a defined benefit plan. The employer alone bears investment risk; employees bear risk only in defined contribution plans.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: hard / L3 — 401(k) loan default deemed distribution
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Angela Torres, age 42, borrowed $40,000 from her 401(k) plan with quarterly repayments over 5 years. After 18 months, Angela's employer terminates her and the outstanding loan balance of $28,000 is offset against her account because she cannot repay. Angela's marginal tax rate is 32%. Applying the plan loan rules under IRC Section 72(p), calculate the total federal tax and penalty on the loan offset.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$11,760 — consisting of $8,960 income tax plus $2,800 early withdrawal penalty", "$8,960 — income tax only, because loan offsets are exempt from the early withdrawal penalty", "$2,800 — only the 10% early withdrawal penalty applies because the loan was tax-free when received", "$0 — the loan offset is not a taxable event because Angela originally received her own money"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 72(p), an outstanding plan loan balance offset upon separation is treated as a distribution. The $28,000 is taxable as ordinary income ($28,000 x 32% = $8,960). Because Angela is under age 59½ and no exception applies, the 10% penalty under Section 72(t) adds $2,800. Total: $11,760.
Wrong (B): Loan offsets are deemed distributions subject to both income tax and the 10% penalty for participants under 59½. No exemption from the penalty exists for loan offsets.
Wrong (C): The early withdrawal penalty applies, but ordinary income tax also applies. The original loan was not taxed, but the offset converts it into a taxable distribution.
Wrong (D): While the loan itself was not taxable when received, the failure to repay triggers treatment as a taxable distribution under Section 72(p).$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — SIMPLE IRA 2-year rollover restriction
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Nathan Webb, age 35, began participating in his employer's SIMPLE IRA plan 14 months ago. He wants to roll his SIMPLE IRA balance into a traditional IRA at a different brokerage. His financial advisor explains the timing restriction. What is the consequence of rolling SIMPLE IRA funds to a non-SIMPLE IRA before the 2-year participation period ends?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The rollover is prohibited and the receiving institution must return the funds within 60 days", "The rollover is treated as a taxable distribution subject to a 25% early withdrawal penalty", "The rollover is permitted but the funds lose their tax-deferred status permanently", "The rollover is treated as a taxable distribution subject to the standard 10% early withdrawal penalty"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 72(t)(6) imposes a 25% early withdrawal penalty instead of the standard 10% on SIMPLE IRA distributions taken within the first 2 years of plan participation if rolled to a non-SIMPLE account. The distribution is also included in gross income.
Wrong (A): The rollover is not mechanically prohibited; it can occur, but it triggers adverse tax consequences including the enhanced 25% penalty.
Wrong (C): A rollover within 2 years does not permanently eliminate tax-deferred status. It is treated as a taxable distribution with the 25% penalty.
Wrong (D): The standard 10% penalty does not apply within the first 2 years. The enhanced 25% penalty under Section 72(t)(6) applies instead.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Coverdell ESA K-12 expense breadth vs 529
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Marcus and Elaine Foster use a Coverdell ESA and a 529 plan for their 12-year-old daughter attending private school. They paid $1,500 for required school uniforms, $2,000 for tutoring, and $800 for school transportation. They ask their CPA which expenses can be paid from each account. Which expenses are eligible for tax-free Coverdell ESA distributions but NOT eligible for 529 plan distributions?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Tuition and required textbooks for K-12 education", "Room and board for a student enrolled at least half-time in college", "School uniforms, tutoring, and transportation required by the school", "Computer equipment used for educational purposes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 530(b)(4), Coverdell ESA qualified elementary and secondary expenses include uniforms, tutoring, and transportation if required by the school. Section 529 qualified K-12 expenses are limited to tuition up to $10,000 per year and do not include uniforms, tutoring, or transportation.
Wrong (A): Both Coverdell ESAs and 529 plans cover tuition for K-12 education. This is not unique to the Coverdell.
Wrong (B): Room and board for college is a qualified expense under Section 529, not a Coverdell-only benefit.
Wrong (D): Computer equipment is a qualified expense under both Section 529 and Coverdell ESA, so it is not unique to the Coverdell.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — 529 non-qualified distribution tax and penalty calculation
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Pauline Clark withdraws $20,000 from her daughter's 529 plan to renovate her home. The account had $60,000 in contributions and $15,000 in accumulated earnings (total $75,000). Pauline's marginal tax rate is 22%. Apply the 529 distribution rules to determine the tax consequences of this non-qualified withdrawal.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The entire $20,000 is taxable as ordinary income at 22%, resulting in $4,400 in tax with no penalty", "Only the earnings portion is taxable with no additional penalty because the funds were used for a personal expense", "The entire $20,000 is subject to both income tax and a 10% penalty, totaling $6,400", "The earnings portion ($4,000) is taxable at 22% plus a 10% penalty on earnings, totaling $1,280"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 529(c)(6), non-qualified distributions are allocated pro rata between contributions and earnings. Earnings portion: $20,000 x ($15,000 / $75,000) = $4,000. Only earnings are taxable ($4,000 x 22% = $880) plus a 10% penalty ($4,000 x 10% = $400). Total: $1,280.
Wrong (A): Contributions to a 529 plan were made with after-tax dollars and are not taxed again. Only the earnings portion is subject to tax.
Wrong (B): While only earnings are taxable, a 10% additional penalty applies to earnings on non-qualified distributions under Section 529(c)(6).
Wrong (C): The contribution portion ($16,000 of the $20,000) is not subject to tax or penalty. Only the $4,000 earnings allocation is taxable with penalty.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — SEP IRA establishment deadline
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Dr. Franklin Boyd, a sole proprietor, had a profitable year in 2025 but did not establish a retirement plan during the year. In February 2026, his CPA advises that he can still set up and fund a plan for 2025. Which retirement plan can Dr. Boyd establish and fund after the end of the tax year?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A SEP IRA, which can be established and funded by the due date of the return including extensions", "A 401(k) plan, which can be established retroactively within 60 days of year-end", "A SIMPLE IRA, which can be established and funded by March 15 of the following year", "A defined benefit plan, which can be established retroactively at any time before the return is filed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 408(k), a SEP IRA can be established and funded up to the employer's tax filing deadline including extensions (October 15 for sole proprietors filing extensions). This makes SEP IRAs uniquely flexible for year-end retirement planning.
Wrong (B): A 401(k) plan must be established by December 31 of the year for which deferrals are made. It cannot be created retroactively after year-end.
Wrong (C): A SIMPLE IRA must be established between January 1 and October 1 of the year. It cannot be set up after year-end for the prior year.
Wrong (D): A defined benefit plan must generally be established by the last day of the plan year, typically December 31 for calendar-year plans.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — SECURE 2.0 enhanced catch-up ages 60-63
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$CPA Howard Blake advises three clients about their 2026 401(k) contributions. Client A is age 52, Client B is age 61, and Client C is age 65. All earn over $350,000 and want to maximize elective deferrals. Under the SECURE 2.0 enhanced catch-up provision, Howard must determine the maximum deferral for each client. Which analysis is correct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All three can defer $31,000 ($23,500 base plus $7,500 catch-up) because catch-up limits are uniform for everyone age 50 and older", "Client A can defer $31,000, Client B can defer $34,750 ($23,500 plus $11,250 super catch-up), and Client C can defer $31,000", "Client A can defer $23,500, Client B can defer $34,750, and Client C can defer $34,750 because anyone over 60 qualifies for the super catch-up", "Client A can defer $31,000, Client B can defer $31,000, and Client C can defer $34,750 because the highest catch-up applies to the oldest participant"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): SECURE 2.0 Section 109 provides an enhanced catch-up of $11,250 instead of $7,500 for participants ages 60 through 63. Client A (52) gets the standard $7,500 catch-up. Client B (61) qualifies for the $11,250 super catch-up. Client C (65) reverts to the standard $7,500 because the enhancement applies only to ages 60-63.
Wrong (A): The enhanced catch-up creates different limits for the 60-63 age group, so catch-up amounts are not uniform for all participants age 50 and older.
Wrong (C): Client A qualifies for the standard catch-up at age 52 ($7,500 additional), not zero. Client C at 65 is outside the 60-63 range and receives the standard catch-up.
Wrong (D): The super catch-up is age-bracketed at 60-63, not applied to the oldest participants. Client C at 65 falls outside this range.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Direct rollover avoids mandatory 20% withholding
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Sophia Tran, age 55, separates from her employer and wants to move her $200,000 traditional 401(k) balance to an IRA. Her plan administrator explains two options: an indirect rollover (check payable to Sophia) or a direct rollover (check payable to the IRA custodian). What is the key tax difference between these two methods?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["An indirect rollover is tax-free, while a direct rollover triggers immediate taxation on the full amount", "Both methods require the plan to withhold 20% for federal income tax", "An indirect rollover requires mandatory 20% withholding; a direct rollover has no withholding requirement", "A direct rollover limits the transfer to $100,000 per year, while an indirect rollover has no dollar limit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 3405(c), eligible rollover distributions paid directly to the participant are subject to mandatory 20% federal income tax withholding. A direct rollover under Section 401(a)(31) bypasses this withholding because the funds never pass through the participant's hands.
Wrong (A): Both methods can achieve a tax-free rollover if completed properly. The difference is in withholding, not ultimate tax liability.
Wrong (B): Only indirect rollovers are subject to mandatory 20% withholding. Direct rollovers specifically avoid this requirement.
Wrong (D): There is no dollar limit on direct rollovers. The full account balance can be transferred directly to the receiving IRA.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Inherited Roth IRA: 10-year rule but tax-free
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$In 2026, Carolyn Peters, age 50, inherits a Roth IRA from her mother, who originally opened the account in 2015 and died in 2026. Carolyn is a non-eligible designated beneficiary. She asks her CPA about the distribution requirements. What rules apply to Carolyn's inherited Roth IRA?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Carolyn must take annual RMDs based on her own life expectancy over an unlimited stretch period", "Carolyn must distribute the entire account within 5 years because Roth IRAs have no required beginning date", "Carolyn must include all distributions in gross income because the 5-year holding period resets for inherited accounts", "Carolyn must empty the account within 10 years, but distributions of both contributions and earnings are tax-free"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 408A(d) and the SECURE Act, non-eligible designated beneficiaries must distribute an inherited IRA within 10 years. Since the Roth IRA was held more than 5 years (opened 2015), all distributions including earnings are tax-free. The 5-year period is measured from the original owner's first contribution.
Wrong (A): The stretch provision based on life expectancy was eliminated by the SECURE Act for most non-spouse beneficiaries. The 10-year rule applies.
Wrong (B): The 5-year rule applies only to non-designated beneficiaries such as estates or charities. Designated beneficiaries follow the 10-year rule.
Wrong (C): The 5-year holding period does not reset upon inheritance. It is measured from the decedent's first Roth IRA contribution year.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Prohibited transaction disqualifies entire IRA
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$CPA Lorraine Becker discovers that her client Derek, age 45, used his self-directed traditional IRA (balance $180,000) to purchase a vacation rental property that Derek and his family personally use for two weeks each year. The IRA trustee was unaware of the personal use. Lorraine must evaluate the consequences of this prohibited transaction under IRC Section 4975. What is the result?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The entire IRA is treated as distributed on January 1 of the year the prohibited transaction occurred, resulting in $180,000 of taxable income plus a 10% early withdrawal penalty", "Only the fair market value of the personal-use portion is treated as a distribution, taxable at ordinary rates with no additional penalty", "Derek must pay a 15% excise tax on the amount involved in the prohibited transaction and remove the property from the IRA within 90 days", "The IRA retains its tax-deferred status, but Derek must pay a 100% excise tax on the personal-use benefit received"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 408(e)(2), if an IRA owner engages in a prohibited transaction, the account ceases to be an IRA as of the first day of the tax year in which the transaction occurred. The entire balance is treated as a distribution, subject to ordinary income tax and the 10% early withdrawal penalty if the owner is under 59½.
Wrong (B): Prohibited transaction consequences are not limited to the portion involved. The entire IRA is disqualified, not just the personal-use component.
Wrong (C): The 15% excise tax under Section 4975(a) applies to qualified plan prohibited transactions, but for IRAs, Section 408(e)(2) imposes the harsher remedy of complete account disqualification.
Wrong (D): The IRA does not retain tax-deferred status after a prohibited transaction. The entire account is deemed distributed under Section 408(e)(2).$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — SECURE 2.0 mandatory auto-enrollment for new 401(k) plans
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Under SECURE 2.0, what is required for new 401(k) plans established after December 29, 2022?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All employees must be enrolled at a fixed 6% default deferral rate with no opt-out provision", "The plan must include automatic enrollment with a default deferral rate between 3% and 10%", "Automatic enrollment is optional but the employer receives a tax credit for adopting it", "Employees must affirmatively elect to participate within 30 days of hire or forfeit eligibility for the year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): SECURE 2.0 Section 101 requires new 401(k) plans established after December 29, 2022, to automatically enroll eligible employees at a default rate between 3% and 10% of compensation, with annual automatic escalation of 1% up to at least 10% and no more than 15%. Employees may opt out.
Wrong (A): Employees always retain the right to opt out of automatic enrollment. The default rate must be between 3% and 10%, not fixed at 6%.
Wrong (C): For new plans established after December 29, 2022, automatic enrollment is mandatory, not optional. Existing plans are grandfathered.
Wrong (D): Automatic enrollment means employees are enrolled by default without affirmative election. They can opt out but do not forfeit eligibility by inaction.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Direct trustee-to-trustee transfer not subject to one-per-year limit
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Laura Meadows, age 48, has traditional IRAs at two different financial institutions. She wants to consolidate both at a single custodian. In 2025, Laura already completed one indirect 60-day rollover between IRAs. What restriction applies to a second IRA-to-IRA transaction in the same 12-month period?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Laura cannot move any IRA funds for the remainder of the 12-month period under any method", "Laura may request a second indirect rollover if she completes it within 30 days instead of 60", "Laura may complete a direct trustee-to-trustee transfer, which is not subject to the one-per-year rollover limit", "Laura must wait until the following calendar year to perform any additional IRA transfers"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 408(d)(3)(B) and the IRS one-rollover-per-year rule, a taxpayer may complete only one indirect 60-day IRA-to-IRA rollover in any 12-month period. However, direct trustee-to-trustee transfers are not treated as rollovers and are unlimited in number and frequency.
Wrong (A): The one-per-year limit applies only to indirect rollovers. Direct trustee-to-trustee transfers are always available as an alternative.
Wrong (B): Shortening the completion window does not create an exception to the one-per-year rule. The limit is on the number of indirect rollovers, not the timeframe.
Wrong (D): The rule is based on a rolling 12-month period, not a calendar year, and it does not restrict direct trustee-to-trustee transfers at any time.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: easy / L2 — 403(b) universal availability requirement
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Westfield Academy, a private school, offers a 403(b) plan. The school wants to limit participation to full-time teachers and exclude part-time staff who work 15 hours per week. The plan administrator reviews the eligibility rules. What is the universal availability requirement for 403(b) plans?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only employees who work at least 30 hours per week must be offered the opportunity to participate", "The employer may exclude any employee earning less than $25,000 annually", "Only tenured or full-time employees must be given access to salary deferrals", "All employees who normally work 20 or more hours per week must generally be given the opportunity to make salary deferrals"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 403(b)(12)(A)(ii) and Treasury Regulation 1.403(b)-5(b), 403(b) plans are subject to a universal availability requirement. If any employee can make salary deferrals, all employees who normally work 20 or more hours per week must be given the same opportunity, with limited exceptions for students and certain part-time workers.
Wrong (A): The threshold is 20 hours per week, not 30 hours. Employees working 20 or more hours must generally be included.
Wrong (B): There is no earnings-based exclusion under the universal availability requirement. Compensation level does not determine eligibility.
Wrong (C): Employment status such as tenured or full-time does not override universal availability. Part-time employees working 20 or more hours must generally be included.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — QJSA default in qualified DB plans
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Roger Bellingham, age 63, is retiring from his company's defined benefit pension plan. He is married to Grace, age 60. Roger wants to elect a single-life annuity for the maximum monthly payment, but the plan administrator says a different form of benefit is the default. Apply the spousal protection rules under IRC Section 417 to determine what Roger must do.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The default is a qualified joint and survivor annuity; Roger must obtain Grace's notarized written consent to waive it and elect the single-life annuity", "Roger may freely elect any payment form without spousal consent because the benefit is based on his own employment", "The default is a single-life annuity for the participant, so Roger does not need to take any additional action", "The plan must offer a lump-sum distribution as the default form and annuity options are elective alternatives"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 417(a) requires that qualified defined benefit plans provide a qualified joint and survivor annuity (QJSA) as the default for married participants. The survivor annuity must be at least 50% of the participant's benefit. To waive the QJSA, the spouse must provide written consent, witnessed by a plan representative or notary public.
Wrong (B): ERISA and IRC Section 417 specifically require spousal consent for waiver of the QJSA. The participant cannot unilaterally elect an alternative payment form.
Wrong (C): The single-life annuity is the default for unmarried participants only. For married participants, the QJSA is the mandatory default.
Wrong (D): Lump-sum distributions are not the required default. The QJSA is the legally mandated default for married participants in defined benefit plans.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — When employer DC contributions are taxed to employee
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$When is an employee taxed on employer matching contributions to a traditional 401(k) plan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["In the year the employer makes the matching contribution to the plan", "In the year the employee receives a distribution from the plan", "Employer matching contributions are never taxable to the employee", "In the year the matching contribution becomes fully vested"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 402(a), employer contributions to a qualified plan are not included in the employee's gross income when contributed. Taxation occurs when the employee takes a distribution from the plan. This deferral is a fundamental benefit of qualified retirement plans.
Wrong (A): A key advantage of qualified plans is that employer contributions are excluded from the employee's income in the contribution year.
Wrong (C): Employer matching contributions are eventually taxable when distributed. The benefit is tax deferral, not permanent tax exclusion.
Wrong (D): Vesting determines whether the employee has a nonforfeitable right to the funds, but vesting alone does not trigger taxation. Tax is triggered only upon distribution.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Roth conversion recharacterization prohibited after TCJA
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$In 2026, William Park converts $50,000 from his traditional IRA to a Roth IRA. By October, the converted amount has declined to $35,000 due to market losses. William asks his CPA whether he can undo the conversion to avoid paying tax on the full $50,000. What option is available under current law?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["William can recharacterize the conversion back to a traditional IRA by the extended filing deadline, restoring the pre-conversion status", "William can reverse the conversion within 60 days as an indirect rollover back to the traditional IRA", "William cannot undo the conversion because the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act permanently eliminated Roth conversion recharacterization after 2017", "William can file an amended return within 3 years to reverse the conversion and receive a refund of the excess tax paid"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017, Section 13611, permanently eliminated the ability to recharacterize Roth IRA conversions for tax years after 2017. William must pay tax on the full $50,000 converted regardless of subsequent market losses. Recharacterization of annual contributions between Roth and traditional IRAs is still permitted.
Wrong (A): Recharacterization of conversions was available before 2018 but was permanently repealed by the TCJA. This option no longer exists for conversions.
Wrong (B): A conversion is not treated as a rollover eligible for 60-day reversal. Attempting this would create an excess contribution to the traditional IRA.
Wrong (D): Amended returns cannot reverse a Roth conversion because recharacterization was the sole mechanism for undoing conversions, and it was eliminated.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Excess IRA contribution: 6% excise tax accumulates annually
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$In April 2025, Nicole Brewer, age 40, contributed $10,000 to her traditional IRA for 2024, exceeding the $7,000 limit by $3,000. She did not discover the error until filing her 2025 return in March 2026, and the $3,000 excess has earned $200. Apply IRC Section 4973 to determine Nicole's cumulative excise tax through the 2025 tax year.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$180 — the 6% excise tax applies only once on the original $3,000 excess", "$192 — the 6% excise tax applies once to the excess plus earnings of $3,200", "$360 — the 6% excise tax applies to $3,000 for 2024 and again to $3,000 for 2025", "$372 — the 6% excise tax applies to $3,000 for 2024 and to $3,200 including net income attributable for 2025"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 4973, a 6% excise tax applies each year the excess remains in the IRA. For 2024: 6% x $3,000 = $180. The uncorrected excess carries forward and grows by the net income attributable ($200), making the 2025 excess $3,200. For 2025: 6% x $3,200 = $192. Cumulative penalty: $180 + $192 = $372.
Wrong (A): The 6% penalty is not a one-time charge. It recurs annually for each year the excess contribution remains uncorrected in the IRA.
Wrong (B): This calculates only one year of penalty on the inflated amount but misses that the penalty applied separately for 2024 as well.
Wrong (C): This correctly identifies two years of penalties but fails to include the net income attributable to the excess in the second year's calculation base.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Annual RMDs required within 10-year period when owner died after RBD
(6, 'Retirement Plans and Education Savings', $EXPL$Mitchell Avery, age 40, inherits a traditional IRA from his father, who died in 2026 at age 76 after having begun required minimum distributions. Mitchell is a non-eligible designated beneficiary. His financial advisor explains the distribution requirements under the SECURE Act's 10-year rule. What annual distribution obligation does Mitchell have?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Mitchell must take annual RMDs in years 1 through 9 based on his single life expectancy, and must empty the account by the end of year 10", "Mitchell has no annual distribution requirement and may withdraw the entire balance at any point within the 10-year period", "Mitchell must withdraw at least 10% of the inherited IRA balance each year over the 10-year period", "Mitchell must take annual RMDs only in years 1 through 5, then may distribute the remainder by year 10"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the SECURE Act and IRS final regulations (TD 9981), when the original IRA owner died after the required beginning date, non-eligible designated beneficiaries must take annual RMDs during years 1 through 9, calculated using the beneficiary's single life expectancy. The entire remaining balance must be distributed by December 31 of the 10th year.
Wrong (B): This describes the rule when the owner dies before the required beginning date. When the owner had already begun RMDs, annual distributions are required within the 10-year window.
Wrong (C): Annual RMDs are calculated using life expectancy factors from IRS tables, not a flat 10% of the account balance each year.
Wrong (D): There is no distinction between the first 5 years and the last 5 years. Annual RMDs are required in each of years 1 through 9, with the remaining balance due in year 10.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
